      "name": {
        "name": "Ras GTPase-activating protein",
        "short": "Ras_GTPase"
      },
      "description": [
        {
          "text": "<p>Ras proteins are membrane-associated molecular switches that bind GTP and GDP and slowly hydrolyze GTP to GDP [[cite:PUB00004087]]. This intrinsic GTPase activity of Ras is regulated by a family of proteins collectively known as 'GAP' or GTPase-activating proteins [[cite:PUB00000983], [cite:PUB00000726]]. RasGAP proteins are usually quite large (from 765 residues for sar1 to 3079 residues for IRA2) but share only a limited (about 250 residues) region of sequence similarity, referred to as the 'catalytic domain' or RasGAP domain. The most conserved region within this domain contains a 15 residue motif which seems to be characteristic of this family of proteins [[cite:PUB00000983]].</p>",
          "llm": false,
          "checked": false,
          "updated": false
        }
      ],
